   :claim "The Part II gate made when rewriting fires endogenous but left which operator fires a run-level property; removing that restriction poses the part's question -- whether the lattice can find its own operating point from runtime-available quantities -- which perturbation reach, requiring a forked twin, cannot answer."
   :title-matches-claim true
   :paragraphs
   [{:n 1 :opening "The gate of \\secref{sec:endogenous-gain} chooses when"
     :role :transition
     :note "States the when/which distinction that the title claims; but its closing move duplicates the part opening (see violations)."}
    {:n 2 :opening "Whether such a lattice can find its own"
     :role :states-claim
     :note "Poses the research question the final section closes, and pre-empts the obvious objection (why not use reach?) with the fork/twin argument -- machinery genuinely needed downstream."}]
   :violations
   [{:quote "This part removes that restriction"
     :kind :redundant
     :suggest "Duplicated framing left by the restructuring: the part opening already made this exact move ('This part takes the remaining step: the operator itself becomes a property of the cell'). Keep the when/which distinction here, and either cut the restatement of the per-cell move ('This part removes that restriction. Each cell carries its own propagator...') down to the vocabulary sentence, or trim the part opening's paragraph 1 to a bare hand-off."}]}

